---
const rows = [
  ["Manner", "How?", "quietly, carefully, well"],
  ["Place", "Where?", "here, outside, nearby"],
  ["Time", "When?", "now, yesterday, soon"],
  ["Frequency", "How often?", "always, often, sometimes, never"],
  ["Degree", "To what extent?", "very, quite, almost"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Adverbs - Adding detail</h1>
  <p>
    An <strong>adverb</strong> can add information about a verb, an adjective or another adverb. It can tell
    how, where, when or how often something happens, or show degree.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
    <h2>Kinds of adverb</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Kind</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Question</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([kind, question, examples]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{kind}</th>
              <td>{question}</td>
              <td>{examples}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p>
    Many manner adverbs end in <strong>-ly</strong>, but not all adverbs do: <strong>often, here</strong> and
    <strong>soon</strong> are adverbs too. Not every <em>-ly</em> word is an adverb:
    <strong>friendly</strong> is an adjective in “a friendly neighbour.” Ask what the word adds, rather than
    trusting the ending.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same word can change jobs. “The children waited <strong>outside</strong>” uses <em>outside</em> as an
    adverb. “They waited <strong>outside the shop</strong>” uses it as a preposition. In “The water was
    <strong>very</strong> cold,” <em>very</em> adds degree to the adjective <em>cold</em>.
  </p>
  <p class="try-it">
    <strong>Try it.</strong> Sort the adverbs in “Yesterday, Aisha carefully placed the box here.”
  </p>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Good, well and fast</h2>
      <p>
        She sings good.
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
        She sings <strong>well</strong>.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
        <em>Well</em> tells us how she sings.
      </p>
      <p>
        She is a <strong>good</strong> singer.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
        <em>Good</em> describes the noun <em>singer</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        He ran fastly.
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
        He ran <strong>fast</strong>.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
        <em>Fast</em> is already an adverb.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
